Republic","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Ange-Félix Patassé"},{"qid":"Q929","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57452","value_label":"François Bozizé","from_t":2003.2,"to_t":2013.2246575342465,"title":"President of the Central African Republic","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"François Bozizé"},{"qid":"Q929","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q8202995","value_label":"Michel Djotodia","from_t":2013.2246575342465,"to_t":2014.0246575342467,"title":"President of the Central African Republic","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Michel Djotodia"},{"qid":"Q929","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q165775","value_label":"Flag of the Central African Republic.svg","from_t":1958.9150684931508,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of the Central African Republic","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of the Central African Republic"}],"people":{"Q57758":{"label":"Faustin-Archange Touadéra","description":"President of the Central African Republic since 2016","enwiki":"Faustin-Archange Touadéra","summary":"Faustin-Archange Touadéra (French: [fostɛ̃ aʁkɑ̃ʒ twadeʁa]; born 21 April 1957) is a Central African politician and mathematician who has been president of the Central African Republic since March 2016, and was previously its prime minister from 2008 to 2013. He was elected to the presidency at the 2015–16 general election, in a second round of voting against former prime minister Anicet Georges Dologuélé. He was re-elected for a second term at the 2020–21 election and a third term at the 2025 Central African election, both of which took place amid low voter turnout and political violence.\nSince at least 2023, Touadéra has been backed by the Wagner Group, a Russian state-sponsored private military company financing his elections and launching disinformation campaigns for him while intimidating his opponents.","image":"Visit of Faustin-Archange Touadéra, President of the Central African Republic, to the European Commission (P067074-430913, cropped).jpg","birth":1957.3013698630136,"death":null},"Q88310768":{"label":"Félix Moloua","description":"Central African Republic politician","enwiki":"Félix Moloua","summary":"Félix Moloua (born 1 June 1957) is a Central African politician and mathematician who has served as the prime minister of the Central African Republic since 7 February 2022. He was the previous minister of planning, economy and cooperation of the Central African Republic. Moloua has a background as a mathematician, physicist and demographer.","image":"Felix Moloua.png","birth":1957.4136986301369,"death":null},"Q15618369":{"label":"Alexandre-Ferdinand Nguendet","description":"Central African politician","enwiki":"Alexandre-Ferdinand Nguendet","summary":"Alexandre-Ferdinand Nguendet (born 23 May 1972) is a Central African politician who served as President of the National Transitional Council of the Central African Republic from 15 April 2013 to 6 May 2016. He briefly served as Acting President of the Central African Republic in January 2014.","image":"Alexandre Ferdinand Nguendet VOA.jpg","birth":1972.3907103825136,"death":null},"Q15633683":{"label":"Catherine Samba-Panza","description":"Central African politician","enwiki":"Catherine Samba-Panza","summary":"Catherine Samba-Panza (née Souga; born 26 June 1954) is a Central African politician who served as Transitional President of the Central African Republic from 2014 to 2016. She was the first woman to serve as head of state in the Central African Republic. Prior to her tenure as acting president, she was the Mayor of Bangui from 2013 to 2014.\nSamba-Panza began her career operating an insurance brokerage and working as a women's rights advocate.","image":"Catherine Samba-Panza 2014-09-26.jpg","birth":1954.482191780822,"death":null},"Q162708":{"label":"Jean-Bedel Bokassa","description":"2nd president (1966–76) and emperor (r. 1976–79) of the Central African Republic","enwiki":"Jean-Bédel Bokassa","summary":"Jean-Bédel Bokassa ([ʒɑ̃ bedɛl bɔkasa] ; 22 February 1921 – 3 November 1996) was a Central African politician and military officer who served as the second president of the Central African Republic (CAR) and Emperor of the Central African Empire (CAE) as Bokassa I, ruling these two states from 1966 to 1979.\nBokassa seized power in the Saint-Sylvestre coup d'état on 1 January 1966. He served about eleven years as president and three years as self-proclaimed Emperor of Central Africa, and although the regime gave the appearance of being a constitutional monarchy, in practice it was an autocratic regime. His imperial regime lasted from 4 December 1976 until his overthrow in a  21 September 1979 coup.","image":"Bokassa portrait.jpg","birth":1921.1424657534246,"death":1996.8387978142077},"Q310860":{"label":"David Dacko","description":"former President of the Central African Republic (1960–1966) and (1979-1981), lived (1930–2003)","enwiki":"David Dacko","summary":"David Dacko (French pronunciation: [david dako]; 24 March 1930 – 20 November 2003) was a Central African politician who served as the first President of the Central African Republic from 14 August 1960 to 31 December 1965 and as the third President of the Central African Republic from 21 September 1979 to 1 September 1981. He also served as Prime Minister of the Central African Republic from 1 May 1959 to 14 August 1960. After his second removal from power in a coup d'état led by General André Kolingba, he pursued an active career as an opposition politician and presidential candidate with many loyal supporters; Dacko was an important political figure in the country for over 50 years.","image":"David Dacko 1962 crop.jpg","birth":1930.2246575342465,"death":2003.8849315068494},"Q310883":{"label":"André Kolingba","description":"Central African politician","enwiki":"André Kolingba","summary":"André-Dieudonné Kolingba (12 August 1936 – 7 February 2010) was a Central African politician, who was the fourth President of the Central African Republic (CAR), from 1 September 1981 until 22 October 1993. He took power from President David Dacko in a bloodless coup d'état in 1981 and lost power to Ange-Félix Patassé in a democratic election held in 1993. Kolingba retained the strong support of France until the end of the Cold War in 1992, after which both internal and external pressure forced him to hold presidential elections which he lost.\nHis twelve-year term in office saw the growing influence of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank in decisions by donor-nations regarding financial support and management of the Central African state.","image":null,"birth":1936.6120218579235,"death":2010.1013698630136},"Q314524":{"label":"Ange-Félix Patassé","description":"former President of the Central African Republic (1937-2011)","enwiki":"Ange-Félix Patassé","summary":"Ange-Félix Patassé (25 January 1937 – 5 April 2011) was a Central African politician who was president of the Central African Republic from 1993 until 2003, when he was deposed by the rebel leader François Bozizé in the 2003 coup d'état. Patassé was the first president in the CAR's history (since 1960) to be chosen in what was generally regarded as a fairly democratic election (1993), in that it was brought about by donor pressure on President André Kolingba and assisted by the United Nations Electoral Assistance Unit.\nHe was chosen a second time in a fair election (1999) as well. However, during his first term in office (1993–1999), three military mutinies in 1996–1997 led to increasing conflict between so-called \"northerners\" (like Patassé) and \"southerners\" (like his predecessor President André Kolingba).","image":"Ange-Félix Patassé (cropped) in 2001.jpg","birth":1937.0657534246575,"death":2011.2575342465752},"Q57452":{"label":"François Bozizé","description":"6th President of the Central African Republic","enwiki":"François Bozizé","summary":"François Bozizé Yangouvonda (born 14 October 1946) is a Central African politician who was President of the Central African Republic from 2003 to 2013. He was the only Central African president born in modern-day Gabon.\nBozizé rose to become a high-ranking army officer in the 1970s, under the rule of Jean-Bédel Bokassa. After Bokassa was ousted, Bozizé served in the government as Minister of Defense from 1979 to 1981 and as Minister of Information from 1981 to 1982.","image":"Francois Bozize.jpg","birth":1946.7835616438356,"death":null},"Q8202995":{"label":"Michel Djotodia","description":"Central African politician and military leader","enwiki":"Michel Djotodia","summary":"Michel Am-Nondokro Djotodia (born 1949) is a Central African politician who was President of the Central African Republic from 2013 to 2014. He was the first Muslim to hold that office in the predominantly Christian country.  Djotodia was a leader of the almost entirely Muslim Séléka rebel coalition in the December 2012 rebellion against President François Bozizé. Following a peace agreement, Djotodia was appointed to the government as First Deputy Prime Minister for National Defense in February 2013.","image":"Michel Djotodia in 2020.jpg","birth":1949,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of the Central African Republic.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Flag_of_the_Central_African_Republic.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Central_African_Republic.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Central_African_Republic.svg","author":"Barthélemy Boganda","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Central African Republic.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Coat_of_arms_of_Central_African_Republic.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Central_African_Republic.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Central_African_Republic.svg","author":"Unknown","license":"Public domain"},"Visit of Faustin-Archange Touadéra, President of the Central African Republic, to the European Commission (P067074-430913, cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Visit_of_Faustin-Archange_Touad%C3%A9ra%2C_President_of_the_Central_African_Republic%2C_to_the_European_Commission_%28P067074-430913%2C_cropped%29.jpg/330px-Visit_of_Faustin-Archange_Touad%C3%A9ra%2C_President_of_the_Central_African_Republic%2C_to_the_European_Commission_%28P067074-430913%2C_cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visit_of_Faustin-Archange_Touad%C3%A9ra,_President_of_the_Central_African_Republic,_to_the_European_Commission_(P067074-430913,_cropped).jpg","author":"Christophe Licoppe / European Communities, 2025 / EC - Audiovisual Service","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Felix Moloua.png":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Felix_Moloua.png/330px-Felix_Moloua.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felix_Moloua.png","author":"U.S. Embassy Bangui","license":"Public domain"},"Alexandre Ferdinand Nguendet VOA.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Alexandre_Ferdinand_Nguendet_VOA.jpg/330px-Alexandre_Ferdinand_Nguendet_VOA.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandre_Ferdinand_Nguendet_VOA.jpg","author":"VOA","license":"Public domain"},"Catherine Samba-Panza 2014-09-26.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Catherine_Samba-Panza_2014-09-26.jpg/330px-Catherine_Samba-Panza_2014-09-26.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catherine_Samba-Panza_2014-09-26.jpg","author":"U.S. Department of State from United States","license":"Public domain"},"Bokassa portrait.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bokassa_portrait.jpg/330px-Bokassa_portrait.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bokassa_portrait.jpg","author":"Jean-Bedel Bokassa &amp; Nicolae Ceaușescu, 1970.jpg: unknown, image comes from the National Archives\nderivative work: Makakaaaa (talk)","license":"Attribution"},"David Dacko 1962 crop.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/David_Dacko_1962_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Dacko_1962_crop.jpg","author":"David Eldan","license":"Public domain"},"Ange-Félix Patassé (cropped) in 2001.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Ange-F%C3%A9lix_Patass%C3%A9_%28cropped%29_in_2001.jpg/330px-Ange-F%C3%A9lix_Patass%C3%A9_%28cropped%29_in_2001.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ange-F%C3%A9lix_Patass%C3%A9_(cropped)_in_2001.jpg","author":"Tanguy Jockmans - European Commission","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Francois Bozize.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Francois_Bozize.jpg/330px-Francois_Bozize.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francois_Bozize.jpg","author":"UNDP/hdptcar","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"Michel Djotodia in 2020.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Michel_Djotodia_in_2020.jpg/330px-Michel_Djotodia_in_2020.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel_Djotodia_in_2020.jpg","author":"VOA","license":"Public domain"}},